<header><p class="enc-kicker">People / group</p><h1 class="enc-title">Claudia gens</h1><p class="enc-meta">Also known as Claudii</p></header>
<div class="enc-body"><p class="enc-desc">The Claudia gens was one of the great patrician families of ancient <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/rome">Rome</a>, said to descend from the Sabine <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/attus-clausus">Attus Clausus</a>, who settled in Rome around 504 BCE. Its members, proud and harsh in Roman tradition, included Appius <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/claudius">Claudius</a> Caecus, builder of the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/appian-way">Via Appia</a>. Through the branch of the Claudii Nerones the family gave Rome the emperors <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/tiberius">Tiberius</a> and Claudius.</p><figure class="enc-fig"><img src="/images/encyclopedia/claudia-gens.jpg" alt="Claudia gens" width="320" height="427" fetchpriority="high"><figcaption>Image: Original version uploaded by George Shuklin on 3 June 2008 · CC BY-SA 1.0 · <a href="https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Bust_of_Tiberius_-_%D0%9F%D0%BE%D1%80%D1%82%D1%80%D0%B5%D1%82_%D0%A2%D0%B8%D0%B1%D0%B5%D1%80%D0%B8%D1%8F_A54,_1_(cropped).jpg" target="_blank" rel="noopener">Wikimedia Commons</a></figcaption></figure></div>
<section class="enc-more"><h2 class="enc-more-h">Origins</h2><p>According to legend the founder of the house, Attius Clausus, led a Sabine faction that wanted peace with <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/rome">Rome</a>; when his efforts failed he went over to the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/roman-empire">Romans</a>, bringing, by the account of Dionysius, no fewer than five hundred men able to bear arms. He took the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/latin">Latin</a> name Appius Claudius, was enrolled among the patricians and given a seat in the Senate. His descendants received a burial site at the foot of the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/capitoline-hill">Capitoline Hill</a>, and his followers land beyond the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/aniene">Anio</a>, which became the core of the Old Claudian tribe. Appius Claudius Sabinus Regillensis was the first of the family to be consul, in 495 BCE.</p><h2 class="enc-more-h">History</h2><p><a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/suetonius">Suetonius</a>, in his life of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/tiberius">Tiberius</a>, counted for the gens twenty-eight <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/consul">consulships</a>, five <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/dictatorship">dictatorships</a>, seven censorships, six triumphs and two ovations. The early Claudii favoured the first names Appius, Gaius and Publius and, by Suetonius's account, avoided Lucius because two early bearers had disgraced the family. Its patrician branches included the Pulchri, Centhones and Nerones, many descended from the sons of Appius Claudius Caecus. Publius Claudius Pulcher, ignoring the auguries, was defeated by the Carthaginian fleet at Drepana in 249 BCE, and Gaius Claudius Nero triumphed over Hasdrubal at the Battle of the Metaurus in 207 BCE.</p><p>Plebeian Claudii appear early, probably descendants of members who passed to the plebs or of the family's <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/freedmen">freedmen</a>. Their branches bore the surnames Asellus, Canina, Centumalus, <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/cicero">Cicero</a>, Flamen, Glaber and Marcellus, and among the Claudii Marcelli Marcus Claudius Marcellus, consul five times between 222 and 208 BCE, was a hero of the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/second-punic-war">Second Punic War</a>. In the late Republic one patrician member voluntarily became a plebeian and adopted the spelling Clodius.</p><h2 class="enc-more-h">Legacy</h2><p>The patrician Claudii were noted for pride and for hatred of the common people, and the historian Niebuhr judged that the house produced several very eminent men but few great ones. No patrician <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/claudius">Claudius</a> adopted a member of another gens during the Republic; the emperor Claudius broke the custom by adopting the future emperor <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/nero">Nero</a>. Claudius also appealed to the family's Sabine origin in arguing before the Senate for admitting <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/gauls">Gauls</a> to it, and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/virgil">Virgil</a> flattered the family in the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/aeneid">Aeneid</a> by making Attius Clausus a companion of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/aeneas">Aeneas</a>.</p><p class="enc-more-src">This account follows the Wikipedia article “Claudia gens” (CC BY-SA 4.0), linked under Read further below.</p></section>
<section class="enc-quotes"><h2 class="enc-section-title">Across the Institute</h2><ul><li><blockquote>Their town Axima (Aime) became Forum <b>Claudii</b> Ceutronum, capital of the small province of Alpes Graiae.</blockquote><a href="/state/ceutrones">Ceutrones</a></li><li><blockquote>The <b>Claudii</b> were said to descend from Attus Clausus, who moved to Rome around 504 BCE.</blockquote><a href="/state/sabines">Sabines</a></li></ul></section>
